From: SkiffOS <skiffos@skiffos.com>
Date: Fri, 04 Apr 2026 20:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] HACK: arm: compressed: sun5i use D-cache write-through during decompression

On Allwinner sun5i (R8/A13, C.H.I.P.) the kernel decompressor hangs
completely after U-Boot jumps to the zImage, producing no output even
with DSB+busy-wait settles added at various points in the decompressor.

Root cause: the decompressor enables write-back D-cache via cache_on()
(see __arm926ejs_mmu_cache_on).  decompress_kernel() then writes ~15 MiB
of decompressed kernel data to DRAM.  The ARM926EJ-S write-back D-cache
buffers these writes and evicts dirty lines to the AHB bus in bursts as
the 8 KB D-cache fills and refills over the course of 15 MiB.

Simultaneously, decompress_kernel()'s ~100 KB code body thrashes the
8 KB I-cache: by the time it returns, the outer decompressor code (the
instruction immediately after "bl decompress_kernel") has been evicted
from I-cache.  That first instruction fetch is an I-cache miss that must
go to DRAM via AHB -- but the AHB/MBUS arbiter is still saturated with
the accumulated dirty-line eviction burst from decompression.  The CPU
stalls indefinitely on that single instruction fetch.

DSB+busy-wait settles added AFTER decompress_kernel cannot help: the
settle code itself is evicted from I-cache and its first instruction
fetch stalls for the same reason.

Fix: in __arm926ejs_mmu_cache_on, enable ARM926EJ-S D-cache write-through
mode (CP15 c15 test register, bit 2) for CONFIG_MACH_SUN5I before calling
__armv4_mmu_cache_on.  Write-through sends each decompressor write directly
to AHB as an individual transaction that the MBUS arbiter drains in real
time.  No dirty-line burst accumulates.  When decompress_kernel() returns,
the AHB has at most a handful of in-flight writes from the last few store
instructions; the first I-cache miss is serviced immediately.

Also clear the write-through bit in __armv4_mmu_cache_off (which is the
cache-off path for ARM926) so that the main kernel starts with normal
write-back D-cache behaviour.  At cache_off time r0 is already zero, so
only the mcr is needed.

The existing settle patches (0003/0006/0007) are retained as defensive
belt-and-suspenders for any residual bus activity, but the write-through
mode change is the primary fix.

Signed-off-by: SkiffOS <skiffos@skiffos.com>
---
 arch/arm/boot/compressed/head.S | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/boot/compressed/head.S
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/compressed/head.S
@@ -877,6 +877,20 @@
 		mov	r0, #4			@ put dcache in WT mode
 		mcr	p15, 7, r0, c15, c0, 0
 #endif
+#ifdef CONFIG_MACH_SUN5I
+		/*
+		 * sun5i AHB/MBUS: enable D-cache write-through during decompression.
+		 * With write-back, decompress_kernel() evicts ~15 MiB of dirty lines
+		 * to the AHB bus.  By the time it returns, the I-cache has been fully
+		 * evicted by the decompressor internal code; the first instruction
+		 * fetch at the return address (I-cache miss) hits a stalled AHB bus.
+		 * Write-through sends each write directly to AHB as individual
+		 * transactions that the MBUS drains in real time -- no burst, no stall.
+		 * This CP15 c15 register is ARM926EJ-S-specific.
+		 */
+		mov	r0, #4				@ ARM926EJ-S WT mode bit
+		mcr	p15, 7, r0, c15, c0, 0		@ enable D-cache write-through
+#endif
 
 __armv4_mmu_cache_on:
 		mov	r12, lr
@@ -1214,6 +1228,10 @@
 		mov	r0, #0
 		mcr	p15, 0, r0, c7, c7	@ invalidate whole cache v4
 		mcr	p15, 0, r0, c8, c7	@ invalidate whole TLB v4
+#ifdef CONFIG_MACH_SUN5I
+		/* clear ARM926EJ-S write-through mode set by cache_on (r0 = 0 here) */
+		mcr	p15, 7, r0, c15, c0, 0
+#endif
 #endif
 		mov	pc, lr
 
